From 73d1ba52a5c24fd12ed1ad41b8b8d5b3080750b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tian Zheng <zhengtian10@huawei.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 18:21:37 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 11/14] KVM: arm64: Derive the VM hardware dirty mode from
 dirty logging

Hardware dirty management of the stage-2 descriptors is currently
enabled or disabled by two independent paths that pull VTCR_EL2 in
opposite directions. Leonardo Bras' "Enable HAFDBS for guests not on
migration" turns HD on outside of migration and off once any memslot
starts logging, so the write-protect permission fault remains the dirty
tracking signal. The HDBSS enablement turns HD|HA|HDBSS on when logging
starts, so hardware promotes writable-clean pages and records the
dirtied IPAs into the per-vCPU buffers instead. Both hooks run from
kvm_arch_commit_memory_region(), so whichever runs second wins, and the
two independent read-modify-write cycles also race against the vCPU
setup path, which could leave HDBSS set with HD clear - an illegal
VTCR_EL2 combination.

Replace both with kvm_arch_update_hw_dirty_mode(), a single derived
mode that is a pure function of the static capabilities and the number
of logging memslots:

  - logging && HDBSS-capable  -> HD|HA|HDBSS (hardware tracking)
  - logging, no HDBSS         -> off (classic write-protect faults)
  - !logging && HAFDBS-cap.   -> HD|HA (only written pages go dirty)

Because the mode is recomputed rather than toggled, there is no
lost-update window to lock against, and a vCPU created while the VM is
migrating inherits the current mode instead of clobbering it.

Only the first vCPU computes the mode from kvm_setup_vcpu(): with no
memslot update having happened yet, nothing else turns the mode bits
on for a fresh VM, while later vCPUs inherit the current mode at their
first vcpu_load() and kvm_arch_commit_memory_region() maintains it
from then on. Skipping them also keeps the vtcr read-modify-write
away from a kvm->lock vs slots_lock mismatch with a concurrent
memslot update.

The HAFDBS leg is not gated on nested virtualization: shadow stage-2
MMUs build their own VTCR through kvm_get_vtcr(), which never carries
the HD bit, so L2 translations keep their software-managed behaviour
while the canonical stage-2 of the guest hypervisor runs under
hardware management. A guest hypervisor cannot reach for the feature
either, as the nested ID registers limit ID_AA64MMFR1_EL1.HAFDBS to
AF-only and the virtual VTCR_EL2.HD is never consumed by the nested
walker. The HDBSS leg stays gated for now: enabling it on the
canonical stage-2 of a nested VM would record L1 dirtiness into the
HDBSS buffer while L2 runs against a shadow, and those exit-flush and
harvest paths are unaudited.

Accordingly, kvm_s2_fault_compute_prot() consults the live HD state of
the MMU being installed into rather than the canonical VTCR: a nested
read fault would otherwise install a writable-clean shadow entry,
which under the HD-less shadow VTCR behaves as read-only and costs L2
an extra permission fault on the first write. kvm_hw_dirty_enabled()
is reshaped into a per-MMU predicate for this.

Always set HA together with HD: FEAT_HDBSS requires
VTCR_EL2.{HDBSS,HA,HD} to be all set, and neither the kernel's own
stage-1 enablement (cpu_enable_hw_dbm()) nor the SMMUv3 driver ever
enables dirty updates without access-flag updates. Hardware-managed AF
also removes the access-flag exits on mmu-notifier aged pages.

The mode switch issues a single KVM_REQ_RELOAD_STAGE2 followed by a
VMID-wide TLB invalidation: the request only reloads VTCR_EL2, while
cached translations created under the old mode survive until
invalidated, mirroring cpu_enable_hw_dbm()'s pairing of the TCR_EL1.HD
flip with local_flush_tlb_all(). In the logging-stop direction (HD
back on) stale read-only translations of writable-clean pages would
otherwise keep faulting until each page takes its next fault.

Turning HD on also makes the hardware promotion of writable-clean
descriptors race with the write-protect walker, so document why the
walker's plain WRITE_ONCE is safe there: hardware only promotes
entries with S2AP[1] clear while the walker only writes entries whose
old value had S2AP[1] set, and the one overlapping window (DBM
removal on writable-clean blocks) discards at most a promotion whose
HDBSS buffer entry and speculative fault-in folio dirtying both
survive the demotion.

kvm_arch_destroy_vm() forces every bit off via
kvm_arm_disable_hdbss_global(), as userspace may destroy the VM without
ever stopping dirty logging.

Also rename kvm_vcpu_has_nv(kvm) to kvm_has_nv(kvm): the predicate is
per-KVM, so the "vcpu" in the name was a lie about its argument.

This is a rework of Leonardo Bras' "Enable HAFDBS for guests not on
migration", which introduced the VHE-only HAFDBS enablement, the
vcpu-setup hook and kvm_set_hafdbs().

